## **4A. Introduction** {.unlisted}

The emerald ash borer (EAB), *Agrilus planipennis* Fairmaire (Coleoptera: Buprestidae), is a flat-headed, wood-boring beetle native to eastern Asia that relies almost exclusively on ash trees (*Fraxinus* species) to complete its life cycle (`\hyperref[ref-poland-2015]{Poland et al. 2015}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-poland-2015">Poland et al. 2015</a>`{=html}, `\hyperref[ref-usda-aphis-2025]{USDA-APHIS 2025}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-usda-aphis-2025">USDA-APHIS 2025</a>`{=html}). It was first detected in North America in 2002 in Michigan and Ontario, although it likely arrived in Michigan during the early to mid-1990s (`\hyperref[ref-siegert-2014]{Siegert et al. 2014}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-siegert-2014">Siegert et al. 2014</a>`{=html}). EAB is now present in 37 U.S. states and six Canadian provinces (`\hyperref[ref-eppo-2025]{EPPO 2025}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-eppo-2025">EPPO 2025</a>`{=html}, `\hyperref[ref-usda-aphis-2025]{USDA-APHIS 2025}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-usda-aphis-2025">USDA-APHIS 2025</a>`{=html}). Adult beetles lay eggs in bark crevices, and upon hatching, larvae burrow into the tree to feed on the inner phloem, outer xylem, and cambium. They overwinter as larvae or pre-pupae within characteristic serpentine galleries beneath the bark (`\hyperref[ref-tluczek-2011]{Tluczek et al. 2011}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-tluczek-2011">Tluczek et al. 2011</a>`{=html}, `\hyperref[ref-poland-2015]{Poland et al. 2015}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-poland-2015">Poland et al. 2015</a>`{=html}). This feeding disrupts the tree’s ability to transport water and nutrients, typically leading to tree death within several years (`\hyperref[ref-knight-2013]{Knight et al. 2013}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-knight-2013">Knight et al. 2013</a>`{=html}). EAB’s cryptic nature, in which all life stages except for the adult beetle are inside of trees, makes early detection of this pest extremely difficult (`\hyperref[ref-haack-2002]{Haack et al. 2002}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-haack-2002">Haack et al. 2002</a>`{=html}, `\hyperref[ref-usda-aphis-2025]{USDA-APHIS 2025}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-usda-aphis-2025">USDA-APHIS 2025</a>`{=html}).

Previous modeling studies indicated broad climatic suitability for EAB across North America that overlapped with the ranges of native *Fraxinus* species (`\hyperref[ref-desantis-2013]{DeSantis et al. 2013}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-desantis-2013">DeSantis et al. 2013</a>`{=html}, `\hyperref[ref-sobek-swant-2012]{Sobek-Swant et al. 2012}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-sobek-swant-2012">Sobek-Swant et al. 2012</a>`{=html}, `\hyperref[ref-liang-fei-2014]{Liang \& Fei 2014}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-liang-fei-2014">Liang &amp; Fei 2014</a>`{=html}, `\hyperref[ref-cuddington-2018]{Cuddington et al. (2018)}`{=latex}, `\hyperref[ref-barker-2023]{Barker et al. 2023}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-barker-2023">Barker et al. 2023</a>`{=html}). All North American ash species are vulnerable to EAB, though blue ash (*F. quadrangulata* Michaux) appears to be less preferred (`\hyperref[ref-poland-2015]{Poland et al. 2015}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-poland-2015">Poland et al. 2015</a>`{=html}). In its native range, the beetle primarily infests stressed or weakened trees, whereas in North America it readily attacks both healthy and stressed individuals, with stressed trees often supporting higher beetle densities (`\hyperref[ref-poland-mccullough-2006]{Poland and McCullough 2006}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-poland-mccullough-2006">Poland and McCullough 2006</a>`{=html}, `\hyperref[ref-tluczek-2011]{Tluczek et al. 2011}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-tluczek-2011">Tluczek et al. 2011</a>`{=html}). Other members of the olive family, such as white fringetree (*Chionanthus virginicus* L.) and olive (*Olea europaea* L.), may also serve as hosts, though survival rates on these species appear to be lower (`\hyperref[ref-rutledge-arango-velez-2017]{Rutledge and Arango-Velez 2017}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-rutledge-arango-velez-2017">Rutledge and Arango-Velez 2017</a>`{=html}, `\hyperref[ref-peterson-cipollini-2020]{Peterson and Cipollini 2020}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-peterson-cipollini-2020">Peterson and Cipollini 2020</a>`{=html}). The economic impact of EAB in the United States has reached billions of dollars, and the widespread loss of ash trees has led to reduced biodiversity and the degradation of important ecosystem services (`\hyperref[ref-kovacs-2010]{Kovacs et al. 2010}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-kovacs-2010">Kovacs et al. 2010</a>`{=html}, `\hyperref[ref-aukema-2011]{Aukema et al. 2011}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-aukema-2011">Aukema et al. 2011</a>`{=html}, `\hyperref[ref-schrader-2021]{Schrader et al. 2021}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-schrader-2021">Schrader et al. 2021</a>`{=html}).

A model for EAB developed for the Degree-Day, establishment Risk, and Phenological event mapping system (DDRP) is operationalized to produce near real-time forecasts for the contiguous United States (CONUS) at <https://uspest.org/DDRP>. The full set of model parameters for EAB, including developmental temperature thresholds, degree-day requirements for individual life stages, and climate stress thresholds and limits, are available at USPest.org (`\hyperref[ref-barker-2023]{Barker et al. 2023}`{=latex}`<a href="#ref-barker-2023">Barker et al. 2023</a>`{=html}). 

## **4B. Results** {.unlisted}

### **i. Phenology**

#### **Entire period: 1981--2025**

Predicted adult emergence and egg hatch for JPB shifted significantly earlier across large portions of CONUS over the entire period (1981–2025), including most East Coast states and parts of the South and Intermountain West (@fig-eab-emergence, @fig-eab-egg-hatch). In these regions, predicted adult emergence and egg hatch decreased by approximately 0.01–0.9 days per year, with some of the largest declines occurring at higher elevations in the Intermountain West. Significant changes were more spatially extensive for predicted egg hatch, encompassing much of the central United States. 


