:::
:::


#### **Recent period: 2001--2025**

Trends in predictions of first adult emergence and egg hatch for EAB over the recent 24-year period (2001–2025) were partially consistent with those observed over the 1981–2025 period. In the East, significant trends were similar in magnitude but less spatially extensive (@fig-eab-emergence-recent, @fig-eab-egg-hatch-recent), whereas most trends in the West were not significant. In contrast to the longer-term trends, parts of the northern Great Plains showed significant shifts toward earlier adult emergence, whereas isolated areas of the interior West, primarily in the southern Intermountain West, exhibited significant trends toward later phenological events.



